==Taxonomy==
First described as ''Echinocactus glaucescens'' in 1828 by [[Augustin Pyramus de Candolle]], the specific epithet "glaucescens" comes from the Latin words "glaucus" for 'blue-green' and "-escens" for 'becoming', referring to the color of the shoots.<ref name="D&Amp (France) 1828 q433">{{cite web | last1=D&Amp | first1=Um National | last2=(France) | first2=histoire naturelle | title=M&#233;moires du Mus&#233;um d'histoire naturelle | publisher=G. Dufour | volume=t. 17 (1828) | date=1828 | url=https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/page/26229182 | access-date=2024-01-21}}</ref> In 1922, [[Nathaniel Lord Britton]] and [[Joseph Nelson Rose]] reclassified it into the genus ''Ferocactus''.
==Cultivation==
In cultivation in [[temperateness|temperate]] regions it must be grown under glass. It has gained the [[Royal Horticultural Society]]'s [[Award of Garden Merit]].<ref>{{cite web|title=RHS Plant Selector - ''Ferocactus glaucescens'' | url=
https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/7165/Ferocactus-glaucescens/Details |accessdate=2 July 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web  | url= https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/pdfs/agm-lists/agm-ornamentals.pdf | title = AGM Plants - Ornamental | date = July 2017 | page = 39 | publisher = Royal Horticultural Society | accessdate = 26 February 2018}}</ref>
There is a spineless [[Form (botany)|form]], ''Ferocactus glaucescens ''forma'' nuda'' (inermis).<ref>[http://www.cactus-art.biz/schede/FEROCACTUS/Ferocactus_glaucescens/Ferocactus_glaucescens_nudum/Ferocactus_glaucescens_nudum.htm ''Ferocactus glaucescens forma nuda'' (inermis)]</ref>
